A=[];ISSUES=[]
def passage(n,s,pages,note):
    A.append(dict(local_id=f'A{n}',statement_original=s,evidence=[dict(page=p,location=f'Auxiliary source passage A{n}') for p in pages],scope_note=note))
def issue(key,text,pages):
    ISSUES.append(dict(issue_id=key,description=text,evidence=[dict(page=p,location='Source scope or notation') for p in pages]))
passage(1,r'''We denote $\rho_*:=P_*(A=0)\in(0,1)$, the true proportion of data from the target population.''',[8],'Observed-target proportion for Theorem 2 and dagger specializations. It is not a positivity assumption on an unobserved target in the general Section 3 setup.')
passage(2,r'''We suppose that $\operatorname{var}_{P_*}(\ell(X,Y)\mid A=0)<\infty$, a very mild condition, throughout this paper.''',[8],'Footnote 2, standing finite target-loss variance. Its X,Y and A=0 wording must be preserved alongside the later generalized Z,Q setting.')
passage(3,r'''In this paper, we define $0/0=0$.''',[8],'Footnote 3 supplies a stated zero-denominator convention; do not report every zero fold count as completely unspecified. It does not define a nonzero numerator divided by zero.')
passage(4,r'''For any non-negative integers $M$ and $N$, we use $[M:N]$ to denote the index set $\{M,M+1,\ldots,N\}$ if $M\leq N$ and the empty index set otherwise; we use $[M]$ as a shorthand for $[1:M]$. For any finite set $S$, we use $|S|$ to denote its cardinality.''',[9],'Finite fold and component ranges, including the empty sums arising when K=1.')
passage(5,r'''For a distribution $P$, we use $P_\sharp$ and $P_{\sharp\mid\natural}$ to denote the marginal distribution of the random variable $\sharp$ and the conditional distribution of $\sharp\mid\natural$, respectively, under $P$; we use $P_{*,\sharp}$ and $P_{*,\sharp\mid\natural}$ to denote these distributions under $P_*$. We use $P^n$ to denote the empirical distribution of a sample of size $n$ from $P$. When splitting the sample into $V>0$ folds, we use $P^{n,v}$ and $P_\sharp^{n,v}$ to denote empirical distributions in fold $v\in[V]$. All functions considered will be measurable with respect to appropriate sigma-algebras, which will be kept implicit. For any function $f$, any distribution $P$, we sometimes use $Pf$ to denote $\int f\,dP$. For any $p\in[1,\infty]$, we use $\|f\|_{L^p(P)}$ to denote the $L^p(P)$ norm of $f$, namely $(\int f(x)^pP(dx))^{1/p}$. We also use $L^p(P)$ to denote the space of all functions with a finite $L^p(P)$ norm, and use $L_0^p(P)$ to denote $\{f\in L^p(P):\int f\,dP=0\}$. All asymptotic results are with respect to the sample size $n$ tending to infinity.''',[9],'Original ambient notation. The printed Lp formula lacks absolute values and an infinity-endpoint definition. L2 and L2_0 are used by the two theorem statements; do not silently rewrite the general notation.')
passage(6,r'''When working under this stronger condition, we use $\mathcal S_k$ to denote $\mathcal S'_k\setminus\{0\}$ for short.''',[10],'Source-only sets exclude the observed target. The preceding stronger condition is DS.0-dagger; S_k can be empty, whereas S-prime_k is nonempty in DS.0.')
passage(7,r'''According to the well-known review by Moreno-Torres et al. [2012], the following four dataset shift conditions are among the most widely considered when one source population is available, so that $\mathcal A=\{0,1\}$. These conditions are all special cases of Condition $\mathrm{DS.0}^\dagger$.''',[10],'Binary one-source context for DS.1 and Section 4. DS.2–DS.4 are not hypotheses of Theorem 2.')
passage(8,r'''If $\theta^{k-1}(\bar z_{k-1})=\infty$, we set $1/(1+\theta^{k-1}(\bar z_{k-1}))$ to be zero. When $\theta^{k-1}$ equals the truth $\theta_*^{k-1}$, this can happen for $\bar z_{k-1}$ outside the support of $\bar Z_{k-1}\mid A=0$ but inside the support of $\bar Z_{k-1}\mid A\in\mathcal S_k$.''',[13],'Footnote 4 fixes the inverse-odds convention at infinity; preserve it for (7), (9), (13) and the dagger algorithm.')
passage(9,r'''We take as given a flexible regression method $\mathcal K$ estimating conditional means and a flexible classifier $\mathcal C$ estimating conditional odds—both taking outcome, covariates, and an index set for data points being used as inputs in order. For example, $\mathcal K$ and $\mathcal C$ may be random forests, neural networks, gradient boosting, or an ensemble learner. We also take as given a flexible density ratio estimator $\mathcal W$, taking an index set for data points being used as input, which may be transformed from a classifier $\mathcal C$ by Bayes’ Theorem or based on kernel density estimators.''',[14],'Given fitting procedures; no particular learner or sufficient smoothness condition is an extra theorem premise. The general algorithm body remains excluded.')
passage(10,r'''Splitting the sample into a fixed number $V$ of folds $I_v$ ($v\in[V]$) leads to the estimating equation $\sum_{i\in I_v}D_{\mathrm{SC}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol\ell}_v,\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}_v,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}_v,r)(O_i)=0$ averaging over data in each every fold $v$, with preliminary estimators $(\widehat{\boldsymbol\ell}_v,\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}_v,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}_v)$ using data outside of the fold. The solution is given in (11).''',[14],'Fixed V asymptotic convention. The source says every preliminary estimate uses out-of-fold data here, but Algorithm 1-dagger line 4 uses in-fold pi. Preserve the discrepancy and the actual algorithm.')
passage(11,r'''It is viable to replace the in-fold estimator $\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}_v$ of $\boldsymbol\pi_*$ with an out-of-fold estimator in Algorithms 1 and $1^\dagger$. These two approaches have the same theoretical properties that we will show next, and similar empirical performance.''',[14],'Remark 2 records an allowed alternative in the general/dagger discussion. This does not erase the different qualification about inconsistent conditional-risk estimation in Section 4 Remark 6.')
passage(12,r'''In the following analyses without assuming Condition DS.0, we assume that nuisance estimators $\widehat{\boldsymbol\ell}_v$ can be evaluated at any point in the space $\mathcal Z$ containing the observation, even if that point is outside the support under $P_*$. For illustration, we assume that, for each $k\in[2:K]$, $\|\widehat\ell_v^k-\ell_\infty^k\|_{L^2(\nu_k)}\xrightarrow{p}0$ for some function $\ell_\infty^k$, where $\nu_k$ denotes the distribution of $\bar Z_k\mid A\in\mathcal S'_k$ under $P^0$.''',[14],'Off-support evaluability is a stated convention. The limiting regressions are illustrative only, as Remark 3 makes explicit. The source writes the otherwise unexplained P^0; do not replace it with P_*.')
passage(13,r'''In all expectations involving nuisance estimators such as $(\widehat{\boldsymbol\ell}_v,\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}_v,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}_v)$, these estimators are treated as fixed and the expectation integrates over the randomness in a data point $O=(Z,A)$. For example, $\mathbb E_{P_*}[\widehat\ell_v^1(Z_1)\mid A=0]=\int\widehat\ell_v^1\,d\mu$ where $\mu$ is the distribution of $Z_1\mid A=0$ under $P_*$, and this expectation is itself random due to the randomness in $\widehat\ell_v^1$.''',[14],'Footnote 5: fitted nuisance functions remain random, but are frozen for integration over a fresh observation. This governs h, B, Delta and the remainder in Theorem 2.')
passage(14,r'''Further, given any scalar $r$, with $\theta^0:=\sum_{a\in\mathcal S_1}\pi^a/\pi^0$, define''',[13],'Generic theta^0 convention preceding (8), also used in (7) and (9). It does not make D_GSC a prerequisite of D_SC.')
